1
00:00:00,340 --> 00:00:02,560
Japanese has three scripts.

2
00:00:02,580 --> 00:00:03,480
But...

3
00:00:03,500 --> 00:00:04,330
why?

4
00:00:04,520 --> 00:00:06,340
Long before it was written down,

5
00:00:06,360 --> 00:00:07,940
Japanese was a living,

6
00:00:07,960 --> 00:00:09,540
spoken language, carried in

7
00:00:09,560 --> 00:00:12,220
conversation, song, and story.

8
00:00:12,240 --> 00:00:13,220
Its deeper origins

9
00:00:13,240 --> 00:00:14,700
are still debated.

10
00:00:14,720 --> 00:00:15,860
But for centuries,

11
00:00:15,880 --> 00:00:18,060
it had no script of its own.

12
00:00:18,080 --> 00:00:20,220
Writing came from across the sea.

13
00:00:20,240 --> 00:00:21,900
Through centuries of contact with

14
00:00:21,920 --> 00:00:23,700
China and the Korean Peninsula,

15
00:00:23,720 --> 00:00:25,020
Chinese characters reached

16
00:00:25,040 --> 00:00:26,620
the Japanese islands.

17
00:00:26,640 --> 00:00:28,420
These characters, kanji,

18
00:00:28,440 --> 00:00:30,060
were designed for Chinese,

19
00:00:30,080 --> 00:00:31,780
a very different language.

20
00:00:31,800 --> 00:00:34,420
So scribes in Japan adapted them:

21
00:00:34,440 --> 00:00:35,740
some for their meaning,

22
00:00:35,760 --> 00:00:37,900
others purely for their sound.

23
00:00:37,920 --> 00:00:39,220
Writing sounds with complex

24
00:00:39,240 --> 00:00:41,020
characters was slow.

25
00:00:41,040 --> 00:00:42,700
As scribes wrote faster,

26
00:00:42,720 --> 00:00:44,700
in flowing cursive brushwork,

27
00:00:44,720 --> 00:00:46,220
the shapes softened.

28
00:00:46,240 --> 00:00:47,060
From these cursive

29
00:00:47,080 --> 00:00:48,860
forms came hiragana.

30
00:00:48,880 --> 00:00:50,900
The kanji "an", for example,

31
00:00:50,920 --> 00:00:52,660
became the hiragana "a".

32
00:00:52,680 --> 00:00:54,660
Hiragana flourished in the poetry

33
00:00:54,680 --> 00:00:56,900
and literature of the Heian court.

34
00:00:56,920 --> 00:00:58,900
Meanwhile, Buddhist scholars needed

35
00:00:58,920 --> 00:01:01,300
quick notes beside Chinese texts.

36
00:01:01,320 --> 00:01:02,740
They took just one piece of a

37
00:01:02,760 --> 00:01:04,380
character, like the left side of

38
00:01:04,400 --> 00:01:07,310
the kanji "i", and created katakana.

39
00:01:07,400 --> 00:01:10,100
Sharp, compact, precise.

40
00:01:10,120 --> 00:01:12,430
Today, all three work together.

41
00:01:12,520 --> 00:01:14,500
Kanji carry core meanings.

42
00:01:14,520 --> 00:01:16,180
Hiragana handle grammar.

43
00:01:16,200 --> 00:01:17,460
Katakana mark foreign

44
00:01:17,480 --> 00:01:19,220
words, and emphasis.

45
00:01:19,240 --> 00:01:20,580
Take this sentence.

46
00:01:20,600 --> 00:01:22,100
Watashi wa nihongo

47
00:01:22,120 --> 00:01:23,460
o benkyou shimasu.

48
00:01:23,480 --> 00:01:25,060
I study Japanese.

49
00:01:25,080 --> 00:01:27,060
Kanji form the core words.

50
00:01:27,080 --> 00:01:28,300
Hiragana link them,

51
00:01:28,320 --> 00:01:29,860
and complete the verb.

52
00:01:29,880 --> 00:01:31,220
Every line of Japanese

53
00:01:31,240 --> 00:01:32,660
carries more than a thousand

54
00:01:32,680 --> 00:01:34,780
years of history: borrowed,

55
00:01:34,800 --> 00:01:36,670
reshaped, and made its own.

56
00:01:36,890 --> 00:01:38,390
And you can still read it on

57
00:01:38,410 --> 00:01:40,470
every street in Japan today.

58
00:01:40,490 --> 00:01:41,670
Three scripts.

59
00:01:41,690 --> 00:01:42,870
One language.

60
00:01:42,890 --> 00:01:45,200
Follow for more Japanese content.

61
00:01:45,400 --> 00:01:49,600
最後まで見てくれてありがとう。またね！
(Thanks for watching till the end. See you!)
